# Release Keyword (clouds-tauri)

Canonical source: `SKILL.md` in this skill root.

Apply this rule when the request mentions `release`, `ship`, `publish`, `package`, `bundle`,
`installer`, `MSI`, `NSIS`, `DMG`, `AppImage`, `sign`, `notarize`, `staple`, `updater`,
`version bump`, `distribute`, `store submission`, or CI that produces artifacts users install.

## Non-negotiables

1. **Signing keys and the updater public key are one-way doors.** The minisign private key
   signs every update; lose it and every installed copy is permanently orphaned — the pubkey
   is compiled into the shipped binary and can only be replaced by an update those users can
   no longer receive. Back it up out-of-band before the first release, and treat rotation as a
   migration, not a config edit. `references/build-and-distribution.md` §Updater: keys,
   artifacts, configuration.
2. **At least two update endpoints, on different infrastructure, from release #1.** Endpoints
   are compiled in. One endpoint is one permanent point of failure for the only mechanism that
   can repair a shipped mistake. `references/build-and-distribution.md` §Updater;
   `references/case-studies.md` §6 for a real app carrying exactly this exposure.
3. **A `204` from the primary endpoint freezes the install base.** `204 No Content` means "you
   are up to date" — it is a *successful* response, so the fallback endpoints are never
   consulted. A misconfigured or hijacked primary that answers 204 silently stops all updates
   forever. Only a hard failure (DNS, connection, 5xx) advances to the next endpoint.
4. **Verify the artifact on a clean machine, not the build machine.** The build machine has
   your toolchain, your certificates, your WebView2 runtime, and your dev keychain. Gatekeeper,
   SmartScreen, missing runtime prerequisites and unstapled notarization are only observable
   elsewhere. `references/build-and-distribution.md` §macOS: sign, notarize, staple, and
   §Windows code signing.
5. **The one-way-door set is bigger than the keypair.** Bundle identifier, Windows
   `upgradeCode`, product name, install path semantics, and the version source of truth all
   become permanent once users install. Run
   `references/build-and-distribution.md` §Release one-way-door checklist before the first
   release, and re-run the marked lines whenever naming, signing, or update infrastructure
   changes.
6. **All target platforms, or say which ones you did not build.** A release is not "done" on
   one OS. `playbooks/cross-platform-validation.md`.

## Order of work

`playbooks/release-preparation.md` drives the sequence; it gates on
`playbooks/security-review.md` and `playbooks/cross-platform-validation.md`. Command-level
lookups: `cheatsheets/build-sign-ship.md`.

## Refuse / escalate

Stop and ask rather than proceed when:

- No backup of the updater private key exists, or nobody can say where it lives. Do not build
  a signed release until that is answered.
- You are asked to change the bundle identifier, `upgradeCode`, or updater pubkey on an app
  that already has installs — surface the migration cost first.
- Only one update endpoint is configured and the user wants to ship anyway. Record it as an
  accepted risk in writing, do not silently proceed.
- Signing credentials, an Apple Developer account, or notarization access are unavailable —
  build unsigned and label the artifact as unsigned rather than pretending it is shippable.
- You cannot test the installer on a clean target. Ship nothing you have only seen run from
  `target/release`.
